<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · artifact</p><h1 class="enc-title">Las Meninas</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1656">1656</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Las Meninas is a painting by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diego-velazquez">Diego Velázquez</a>, completed in 1656 and now in the Prado in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a>. It shows the Infanta Margarita Teresa with her maids of honour and attendants in the painter's studio, with Velázquez himself at his easel and the king and queen reflected in a mirror. Its complex play of viewpoints has made it one of the most studied paintings in Western art.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/las-meninas.jpg" alt="Las Meninas" width="320" height="368" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Diego Velázquez · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Las_Meninas,_by_Diego_Vel%C3%A1zquez,_from_Prado_in_Google_Earth.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>In 17th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> painters rarely enjoyed high status, since painting was regarded as a craft rather than an art such as poetry or music. Velázquez nonetheless rose through the court of Philip IV and in February 1651 was appointed palace chamberlain, a post that brought status but left him few works in his last eight years, mostly portraits of the royal family. The king allowed him to include himself in Las Meninas although he resisted being portrayed in old age. In the early 1650s Philip gave Velázquez the main room of the late Balthasar Charles's quarters, then the palace museum, as a studio; the painting is set there, in the Royal Alcázar of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The five-year-old Infanta Margaret Theresa, then the only surviving child of Philip IV and Mariana of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, stands in the centre foreground. Two ladies-in-waiting, or meninas, attend her: Doña Isabel de Velasco, who is about to curtsy, and Doña María Agustina Sarmiento de Sotomayor, who kneels to offer her a drink. To the right are the dwarfs Mari Bárbola and Nicolás Pertusato, who nudges a mastiff with his foot, and the chaperone Doña Marcela de Ulloa speaking to a bodyguard. Behind them Velázquez portrays himself at a large canvas, and a mirror at the back reflects the upper bodies of the king and queen.</p><p>Nine figures appear, eleven counting the reflections, yet they fill only the lower half of the canvas. Three figures, Velázquez, the infanta and Mari Bárbola, appear to look straight at the viewer. The mirror places the king and queen on the viewer's side of the picture, although some scholars have speculated that their image is a reflection of the painting Velázquez is working on. The painting has been cut down on both sides and was damaged in the 1734 fire that destroyed the Alcázar; the infanta's left cheek was almost entirely repainted afterward.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Early inventories called the painting La Familia, and it entered the Prado at its founding in 1819; the Prado catalogue first listed it as Las Meninas in 1843. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luca-giordano">Luca Giordano</a> described it as the theology of painting, and in 1827 Sir Thomas Lawrence called it the true philosophy of the art. Michel Foucault opened The Order of Things with a long analysis of it. Svetlana Alpers reads it as Velázquez's claim that painting is a liberal rather than a mechanical art, a point that mattered because the rules of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/order-of-santiago">Order of Santiago</a> excluded those in mechanical occupations. Goya etched a print of it in 1778 and modelled Charles IV of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and His Family on it.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Las Meninas” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…de Cervantes’s Don Quixote (1605–1615), the plays of Lope de Vega and Pedro Calderón de la Barca, the poetry of Luis de Góngora and Francisco de Quevedo, and the paintings of El Greco, Diego Velázquez, whose <b>Las Meninas</b> dates from 1656, Francisco de Zurbarán and Bartolomé Esteban Murillo.</blockquote><a href="/empire/habsburg-spain">Habsburg Spain · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
